# Requirements you can check

### EARS, Given/When/Then, closure

Tutors Ladder, rung 5 (Proven), talk 2

---

## A requirement nobody can check

> The generator should handle lab folders properly.

- "should": is it required or not?
- "handle", "properly": what would you observe?
- Which code does it bind? Which test proves it?

An agent reading this will do something. You cannot tell whether it did the right thing.

---

## EARS: five sentence shapes

| Pattern | Reads |
| --- | --- |
| Ubiquitous | The \<system\> shall \<response\>. |
| Event-driven | When \<trigger\>, the \<system\> shall \<response\>. |
| State-driven | While \<state\>, the \<system\> shall \<response\>. |
| Optional | Where \<feature\>, the \<system\> shall \<response\>. |
| Unwanted behaviour | If \<condition\>, then the \<system\> shall \<response\>. |

Easy Approach to Requirements Syntax. One "shall". A named system. Something you can observe.

---

## The same rule, in EARS

```
R-004 | Event-driven |
When a lab folder holds markdown files, the generator shall write one step per file,
in file-name order, whose id is the file name between its first and last dot. | gen
```

- The trigger: a lab folder with markdown files
- The system: the generator, which lives in `packages/gen`
- The response: something a test can compare exactly

`variants/rung-5-proven/REQUIREMENTS.md` holds twelve of these, each naming its layer.

---

## Given / When / Then

```gherkin
@R-004 @ears-event-driven
Rule: When a lab folder holds markdown files, the generator shall write one step per file, ...

  Scenario: The steps of a lab
    Given a course with a lab "topic-1/book-1" holding the files "02.Build.md, 00.Lab-1-Intro.md, 01.Set.Up.md"
    When the course is built
    Then the lab "book-1" has the steps "Lab-1-Intro, Set.Up, Build"
    And each step of the lab "book-1" has the lab's route followed by its id
```

The `Rule:` title is the requirement, word for word. The scenarios are its proof.

---

## Binding the steps

```ts
// tests/bdd/steps/generator/course-folder.steps.ts
const feature = await loadFeature("tests/bdd/features/generator/course-folder.feature");

describeFeature(feature, ({ BeforeEachScenario, Rule }) => {
  Rule("When a lab folder holds markdown files, the generator shall write ...", ({ RuleScenario }) => {
    RuleScenario("The steps of a lab", ({ Given, When, Then, And }) => {
      Given("a course with a lab {string} holding the files {string}", givenLab);
      When("the course is built", build);
      Then("the lab {string} has the steps {string}", (_ctx, id, steps) => { ... });
```

vitest-cucumber fails if a Rule, scenario or step exists on one side only.

