```

```ts
// tests/bdd/steps/generator/course-json.steps.ts
const feature = await loadFeature("tests/bdd/features/generator/course-json.feature");
describeFeature(feature, ({ Background, Rule }) => { ...
```

Same library, same shape. Upstream ids are `@rule-NNNN`; rung 5 uses `@R-NNN` and a table.

---

## Closure: two lists that must agree

```
REQUIREMENTS.md            every id ──────► at least one Rule with a scenario
tests/bdd/features         every Rule ────► exactly one known id, its sentence word for word
any test title             "R-008: ..." ──► an id that exists
```

If any arrow breaks, `pnpm test:ears:audit` names it:

```
unproved-requirement: REQUIREMENTS.md:24 R-013: no Rule tagged @R-013 has a scenario.
  Add one to the feature for its layer in tests/bdd/features.
rule-title-drift: tests/bdd/features/site/static-site.feature:48: the Rule's title is
  not R-011's sentence. One sentence, one source: copy it from REQUIREMENTS.md:22 word for word.
```

---

## Checking the sentence itself

`scripts/checks/ears-audit.ts` reads each requirement and fails on:

| Code | Example |
| --- | --- |
| `shall-count` | two "shall"s: split it |
| `obligation-keyword` | "should", "must", "will" |
| `vague-language` | "handle", "properly", "quickly" |
| `system-name` | the word before "shall" is not the generator, the site or the command |
| `ears-form` | Event-driven but does not start "When ...," |
| `layer-mismatch` | about the site, but "Implemented in" says gen |
| `ears-tag` | the Rule's `@ears-*` tag is not its pattern |

---

## How upstream audits rule ids

`scripts/checks/ears-audit.ts` upstream checks every `Rule:` in 85 files:

- `rule-id`: exactly one `@rule-NNNN` tag; `--next-id` prints the next free one (`0786` at `2b96b69`)
- `rule-id-retired`: ids in `tests/bdd/ears-retired-rule-ids.txt` are never reused
- `shall-count`, `obligation-keyword`, `vague-language`, `system-name`, `ears-form`, `ears-tag-*`
- `dual-scenarios`: a state-driven or optional Rule needs an `@active` and an `@inactive` scenario
- `unbound-feature`, `unbound-rule`: no steps file loads it
- `unproved-scenario`, `orphan-ui-test`: for `@ui` Rules proved by Playwright

It is pure functions; `tests/suite-health/ears-audit.test.ts` proves each code on fixtures.

---

## The house style it borrows from

`AI-Reference-Projects/typescript` keeps requirements in a table too:

```ts
// typescript/tests/architecture/requirements.test.ts
expect([...required].filter((id) => !cited.has(id)), "add a test whose title opens with each id").toEqual([]);
expect([...cited].filter((id) => !required.has(id)), "state the rule in REQUIREMENTS.md first").toEqual([]);
```

Rung 5 joins the two ideas: the table, and Rules that repeat it word for word.

---

## Why this matters with an agent

- The requirement is what a person approves. It is short enough to read.
- The agent cannot add behaviour without a sentence, or a sentence without proof: the closure fails.
- Upstream's `.claude/skills/ears-gherkin-dev/SKILL.md` makes this the workflow: write the Rule, show it to
  the human and "wait for a yes", prove it red, implement, prove it green, audit.

---

## Take away

- One sentence, one id, one source. Repeat it; never paraphrase it.
- A Rule without a scenario is a wish. A scenario without a Rule cites nothing.
- Check the shape of the sentence as well as the behaviour.
- Next: how to tell whether the tests themselves can be trusted.
